    <!-- The theme ships its own fonts in Typefaces/, but reaching them is awkward.

         The directory is NOT called Fonts/ on purpose. Toolbox's packer blacklists
         "^Fonts\\" (Playnite.Toolbox/Themes.cs, PackageFileBlackListRegex) because its
         scaffolder copies Playnite's own common fonts there, so anything a theme puts in
         Fonts/ is silently dropped from the .pthm - it works when deployed by hand and
         vanishes the moment the theme is packaged. Renaming the folder is the whole fix;
         the blacklist is anchored at the start of the relative path.

         Playnite parses theme XAML with Xaml.FromFile -> XamlReader.Load(stream) with no
         ParserContext, and only assigns ResourceDictionary.Source afterwards. By then the
         FontFamily objects already exist with a null BaseUri, so a theme-relative path
         ("Typefaces/#Inter") has nothing to resolve against and lands on the Playnite
         install root instead. {ThemeFile} can't rescue it either: it runs the target
         property's TypeConverter (right shape for FontFamily) but File.Exists-checks the
         relative path first, and the "#Family" fragment WPF requires makes that check fail.

         The path segment "VibeMythos_fb4d738f-..." is theme.yaml's Id - Playnite derives the
         deployed folder name from it. Change the Id and all three chains below silently
         stop resolving entry 1. CLAUDE.md carries a validator for this.

         So each entry below is a comma-separated fallback chain, most specific first:
           1. install-root-relative path to our bundled file  (portable installs under
              <PlayniteInstall>\Themes\Desktop\; misses under %AppData%\Playnite\Themes\)
           2. the bare family name                            (works if the user has it installed)
           3. a system face                                   (always resolves)
         A chain entry that fails resolution is skipped, so the worst case is exactly the
         behaviour we had before shipping any fonts - never a hard fall to Arial.

         Note step 2 is weaker than it looks: WPF family matching is fuzzy, so a bare
         "Inter Tight" or "Inter Mod" silently resolves to plain "Inter" when only Inter is
         installed. That is the bug this workstream exists to fix, and it is why a fallback
         chain alone was never sufficient - the files have to ship. It also means a bare
         "Inter" entry placed AFTER "Inter Tight"/"Inter Mod" is dead weight, and anything
         after that is unreachable on a machine with Inter present - so the chains below
         list only entries that can actually be selected.

         Do NOT bind any of these to HtmlTextView.HtmlFontFamily. That control stringifies
         FontFamily.ToString() into a *quoted* CSS font-family (Playnite/Controls/
         HtmlTextView.cs), so the whole chain would be read as one family name and match
         nothing. Use HtmlDescriptionFontFamily below. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamily">Themes/Desktop/VibeMythos_fb4d738f-62bd-4e08-afd9-52e8cb45f6ca/Typefaces/#Inter, Inter, Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <!-- Misnomer kept for compatibility: "Inter Mod" is a modified Inter, not a monospace,
         and both consumers are prose (the Notes box and the HTML description). Terminal
         entries are therefore a prose stack, not Consolas. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="MonospaceFontFamily">Themes/Desktop/VibeMythos_fb4d738f-62bd-4e08-afd9-52e8cb45f6ca/Typefaces/#Inter Mod, Inter Mod, Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <!-- HtmlTextView renders through HtmlRenderer, which only sees system-installed fonts -
         the bundled files are unreachable there - and it injects this value into a quoted
         CSS declaration. It must stay a SINGLE family name: no path, no commas. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="HtmlDescriptionFontFamily">Inter</FontFamily>
    <!-- Segoe Fluent Icons is Win11; Segoe MDL2 Assets is the Win10 fallback. Centralized
         here so the 63 glyph sites don't each carry the pair as a literal. Unlike the
         chains above this is a genuine CSS-style pair of system faces, not a path. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="IconFontFamily">Segoe Fluent Icons, Segoe MDL2 Assets</FontFamily>

